   "text": "<<The Riddle of the Door>>\n\nI AM EASY TO GET INTO BUT HARD TO GET OUT OF.  \nThis is a common riddle that describes a situation where entry is simple, but exit is difficult. It often refers to a place or situation that is accessible but not desirable to leave. For example, a room that is easy to enter but hard to leave, or a situation that is convenient to start with but becomes burdensome to leave.\n\n***  \n\nI AM WHERE YOU NEED TO BE BUT YOU MAY NOT WANT TO STAY THERE.  \nThis phrase describes a place that is essential for your needs but may not be pleasant to be in. It could refer to a room that is necessary for your work or rest, but you might not want to be there all the time. It can also be used metaphorically to describe a situation that is required but not desired.\n\n***  \n\nI AM THE PLACE WHERE YOU CAN FIND ME BUT NOT Always.  \nThis is a more abstract version of the riddle, suggesting that the place is present but not constant. It could be a room that is always there but not always desirable, or a situation that is always present but not always wanted. It emphasizes the duality of presence and absence.",
